\b 1. Agent Environment and PEAS\b0\line
\line
\b Problem Context\b0\line
A travelling agent must move from Chennai (node A) to Hyderabad (node X) on a weighted graph. Edge weights represent approximate travel time between cities. The task is to identify a valid route to the goal using the required search methods (IDA* and Hill Climbing), while reporting path and complexity.\line
\line
\b PEAS Description\b0\line
\b Performance Measure:\b0 Reach goal node X with minimum travel cost; produce valid path; report time and space complexity.\line
\b Environment:\b0 Weighted graph of cities, static during one run, fully observable.\line
\b Actuators:\b0 Move from current node to an adjacent node connected by an edge.\line
\b Sensors:\b0 Current node, neighboring nodes, and edge costs from graph representation.\line
\line
\b Environment Characteristics\b0\line
- Fully observable: all nodes/edges/costs are known from the start.\line
- Deterministic: moving on an edge always gives the same next node and cost.\line
- Sequential: each decision affects subsequent choices in the path.\line
- Static: topology and weights do not change during execution.\line
- Discrete: nodes and transitions are finite and countable.\line
\line
\b 2. Heuristic / Fitness Function Definition\b0\line
\line
For this problem, the heuristic follows the assignment statement exactly:\line
\line
h(n) = average of total travel costs across all simple paths from node n to goal node X.\line
\line
Interpretation:\line
- Lower h(n) indicates that node n is generally closer (in travel-time sense) to X.\line
- The heuristic is computed from graph structure, not manually guessed.\line
\line
Usage in algorithms:\line
- IDA*: uses f(n) = g(n) + h(n), where g(n) is path cost from start to n.\line
- Hill Climbing: selects neighbor with best (lowest) heuristic value; if no improving neighbor exists, local optimum behavior is reported.\line
\line
\b 3. Data Structures Used in Implementation\b0\line
\line
- Graph: Dictionary of dictionaries (adjacency map with edge weights).\line
Example: graph[\'A\'][\'D\'] = 3\line
\line
- Path containers: list for ordered path sequence.\line
- Visited tracking: set to prevent node revisits in simple-path exploration.\line
- Heuristic table: dictionary mapping node -> heuristic value h(n).\line
- Result object: structured record containing path, cost, expanded nodes, depth, and flags.\line
\line
\b 4. Informed and Local Search Methods (Summary)\b0\line
\line
\b IDA* (Informed Search)\b0\line
- Starts with threshold = h(start).\line
- Performs depth-first bounded search using f(n).\line
- If goal not found, threshold increases to minimum exceeded f-cost and repeats.\line
- Returns path A -> D -> G -> X (cost 19) in the submitted implementation run.\line
\line
\b Hill Climbing (Local Search)\b0\line
- At each step chooses best heuristic neighbor.\line
- Tracks local optimum condition when strict heuristic improvement is not possible.\line
- Submitted run reports path A -> D -> G -> X and marks local-optimum encountered as Yes.\line
\line
\b 5. Complexity Reporting Approach\b0\line
\line
The code prints complexity information explicitly:\line
- IDA*: Time O(b^d) worst case, Space O(bd) worst case.\line
- Hill Climbing: Time O(b) per move, Space O(b) for neighbor inspection.\line
